this is what remains of a 12-story  apartment block on florida's atlantic  coast  it happened while they were sleeping at  1 30 a.m  people nearby thought it could have been  a piercing crack of thunder  the truth was far more traumatic  a cctv camera capturing the moment the  building came  down with no warning for dozens of  people inside  apartment units ripped in two by the  force  lives torn apart we just toured  around the complex to be able to see  and the tv doesn't do it justice i mean  it is really really  uh traumatic to see the collapse of a  massive structure like that  one person died 10 were injured 99  are still unaccounted for for those  waiting to hear from loved ones  it's unimaginable just today friends of  the family had decided to stay the night  of all days they chose the worst one to  stay there and then this happened  they were trying to get away from the  covid situation in argentina which is  really complicated at this time  it's a family that's worked really hard  their entire lives  they were finally able to adopt their  young daughter after years of much  sacrifice and now  the three of them were in there we have  friends who have family that live in the  building  you know we don't even know if they're  okay some of them are okay  we don't know if the rest are okay we  really like it's just you know like  it's like it's very shocking  there were 134 apartment units in this  building  more than half were destroyed from the  side  still standing residents were winched to  safety  some had been barricaded inside their  homes by the sheer weight of the debris  there was initially hope as this young  boy was carried from the rubble by  firefighters  who began the rescue operation despite  the risk of the entire building  collapsing  sniffer dogs are being used to detect  signs of life  this community is mostly home to  families  retirees and those seeking sunshine on  florida's sandy beaches  the search and rescue operation  continues  the inquiry into why this building came  down with  such devastating consequence has already  begun  martha kellner sky news florida
 

